      <td class="abe-column-block-padding" id="abe-column-block-dc99f2c0-bfea-4bba-a3ad-dc69080610a3-padding" style="-webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; -ms-text-size-adjust: 100%; mso-table-lspace: 0pt; mso-table-rspace: 0pt; padding: 0px 5px 5px 10px; border: none;">
<p style="text-align: left; -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; -ms-text-size-adjust: 100%; line-height: 1.4; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 14px; font-family: helvetica; margin: 0 0 15px;" align="left"><strong><span class="a_GcMg font-feature-liga-off font-feature-clig-off font-feature-calt-off text-decoration-none text-strikethrough-none" style="line-height: 1.4;">PROSPERITY</span><span class="a_GcMg font-feature-liga-off font-feature-clig-off font-feature-calt-off text-decoration-none text-strikethrough-none" style="line-height: 1.4;">: </span>Southwest Community Plan</strong> - Mayor Ginther launched development of a new community plan for the Southwest Side focusing on Census Track 51. Over the next 10-to-12-months, the Department of Neighborhoods, in collaboration with Columbus Public Health, will engage the community to develop a plan focusing on improving health, safety, transportation, job creation, and educational equity in historically underserved neighborhoods.</p>
<img class="govd_template_image" src="https://content.govdelivery.com/attachments/fancy_images/OHCCC/2026/06/13938338/6979160/southwestplan2_crop.jpg" style="width: 585px; height: auto; -ms-interpolation-mode: bicubic; line-height: 1; outline: none; text-decoration: none; max-width: 100%; border: none;" alt="IMAGE" width="585" height="186"><p style="-webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; -ms-text-size-adjust: 100%; line-height: 1.4; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 14px; font-family: helvetica; margin: 1em 0 15px;"><span data-teams="true" style="line-height: 1.4;"><strong>CULTURE:</strong> <strong>Living the Core Values of Unity and Community</strong> – The City of Columbus recognized and celebrated Juneteenth and Pride! Hundreds of city team members walked in this year’s Pride Parade, and an event was recently held to illuminate City Hall in red and green to recognize and honor Juneteenth.</span></p>
<p style="-webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; -ms-text-size-adjust: 100%; line-height: 1.4; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 14px; font-family: helvetica; margin: 0 0 15px;"><strong>OPERATIONS: Monthly Strategic Session</strong> – The June meeting included an update from the Office of Diversity and Inclusion as well as completion of the Shared Opportunities Exercise. The ideas identified and prioritized by the group through the exercise will guide the focus of the Strategic Sessions for the remainder of the year.   </p>
<p style="-webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; -ms-text-size-adjust: 100%; line-height: 1.4; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 14px; font-family: helvetica; margin: 0 0 15px;"><strong>CULTURE:</strong> C<strong>hange Support Network</strong> – The group met on June 24th to focus on addressing cultural challenges and developing actionable citywide initiatives. The ideas generated will help create meaningful advancements that we aim to implement by the end of the year.</p>
</td>
    </tr>
